1. a3 f5 2. d4 Nf6 3. c4 g6 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Qc2 d6 7. Bg5 c6 8. Rd1 Qe8 9. d5 Nbd7 10. Nd4 Nb6 11. e3 Kh8 12. Be2 e5 13. dxe6 Bxe6 14. Nxe6 Qxe6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qd3 Rad8 17. O-O d5 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Bf3 Nc4 20. Qc2 d4 21. exd4 Bxd4 22. Rfe1 Qf7 23. h3 Ne5 24. Be2 f4 25. Qe4 Qg7 26. Bf3 Nc6 27. Qc2 Rc8 28. Bxc6 Rxc6 29. Rd3 Rfc8 30. Rc1 Qe5 31. Qd2 Rc4 32. Re1 Qc5 33. Ne4 Qb6 34. Re2 Be5 35. Nc3 Qf6 36. Rd7 Bxc3 37. bxc3 Qxc3 38. Qxc3+ Rxc3 39. Ree7 {"Here was perhaps John's greatest upset against the eventual tournament winner. During a time scramble both fell now fall, with Littlewood and Howson both having one more move to make. To the onlookers it appeared as though Littlewood still had a minute left whilst Howson only had a fraction of a second (certainly not more than ten seconds). Littlewood, wishing to avoid acrimony, sportingly proposed to play out their last move each without regard to the clock. Howson consequently had some twenty valuable minutes for the last move before the time control!" (Keith Brown) Source: John Littlewood collection (Keith Brown)} 1/2-1/2

